module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.instruction_and_subject_resolved_from_different_revisions

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data instruction_and_subject_resolved_from_different_revisions: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "instruction_and_subject_resolved_from_different_revisions" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "**a step's INSTRUCTION and the TREE it operates on are resolved from different revisions** (INVALID STATE: the command a step runs -- which entry point, which function, which path -- is taken from revision A, while the working tree that command is pointed at is checked out at revision B, and nothing in the step binds the two or reports which is which. HARM: the split has TWO ARMS AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME RUNG. LOUD ARM: the step's refusal is REAL, typed and located, and it names a subject that never carried the instruction; Section 5's located diagnostic is intact and points at the wrong tree, so the repair it invites -- edit the module the error names -- is correct-looking and cannot work, because that module is not what disagreed. SILENT ARM: the step does not refuse at all. When the subject revision lacks the instruction's NEW PART rather than the entry point itself, the older subject still answers the command and prints a counts shape the instruction never specified, and the step exits 0. The lane is then green over a subject that was never judged by the instruction the run executed.",

    "THIS IS NOT A STALE-BASE PROBLEM AND CALLING IT ONE SENDS THE FIX TO THE WRONG PLACE. Age of the base is neither necessary nor sufficient: a branch whose instruction and tree both come from the OLD revision is perfectly consistent and refuses nothing, and a branch that integrates the new revision is consistent again. What produces the failure is the SPLIT, and a step can hold it open at any base age. The reason it presents as staleness is that the split is invisible while the two revisions happen to agree, which is most of the time.",

    "**SPECIMEN, gunbc#10479, run 33926616205 job 101196425514 (2026-09-04).** The heal-generated-artifacts job refused with NoSuchFunction { name: heal_repair_declaration } while evaluating dag/gunbc/instruments/generated_artifact_gate.dag. The function and the invocation of it landed together in ONE commit, 865fddf03d (#10373) -- .dag definition and workflow argv in the same diff -- so the pair is atomic everywhere and no asymmetric merge resolution is involved. The split is the job's own construction: for a pull_request event GitHub supplies the WORKFLOW FILE from the merge ref, and this job declares ref: github.head_ref because it PUSHES the regeneration back to the branch and therefore cannot run detached on refs/pull/N/merge. Its checkout line reads git checkout --progress --force -B <branch> refs/remotes/origin/<branch>, not a merge ref. Measured at three revisions: branch head a86d7ed225 had definition 0 and invocation 0, a consistent OLD pair; origin/main had 1 and 1, a consistent NEW pair; what EXECUTED was the invocation from the merge ref against the definition from the branch head. git merge-base --is-ancestor 865fddf03d a86d7ed225 is false. Every other required job runs the merge ref and stayed green, which is why exactly one job reddened and why integrating main cleared it with nothing resolved by hand.",

    "**AND THE FIRST ACCOUNT OF THIS SPECIMEN WAS WRONG IN THE INSTRUCTIVE DIRECTION, which is why the population statement is written twice here.** It was first filed as any branch based before 865fddf03d will red this way -- a rule keyed on the BASE. A reviewer then established that both halves landed atomically and concluded the opposite, that a consistent old pair cannot refuse and so the cause must be an asymmetric merge resolution that took one side. Both readings check one ref and infer the other. The measurement that decides it is the checkout line in the job log, and neither account had read it. The population is: the branch HEAD predates the commit that added the invocation, AND the entry point is reached by a step that checks out the head rather than the merge ref -- narrower than base age in one direction, and broader than a bad merge resolution in the other.",

    "**SECOND SPECIMEN, gunbc#10490, AND IT IS A MATCHED PAIR RATHER THAN A SECOND SIGHTING.** Reached independently by another lane on a case neither party to the first diagnosis chose, and diagnosed correctly there before this row existed -- which is the evidence the roster is for: the class is recognisable in the wild, not only in retrospect. Re-measured here rather than accepted as reported. RED ARM, head c30c049f68: the definition is absent (git merge-base --is-ancestor 865fddf03d c30c049f68 is false), and on run 33930364431 heal-generated-artifacts failed while required-witnesses-build, required-witnesses-floor and the aggregator all succeeded -- one job red, and it is the one job whose ref differs from the workflow file it is running. GREEN ARM, head 5888fb0106 after integrating main: the definition is present. The property flips with the remedy, which makes the pair discriminating rather than illustrative, and per section 4b(4) it stays enrolled as the arm that would catch a regression in the eventual fix. ONE HONEST GAP IN THAT PAIR: at the time of writing, #10490 green run 33932538855 had not concluded, so the executed green arm is gunbc#10479 at 245416762b, where heal passed in 18m56s after exactly this remedy. Naming #10490 as an executed positive control before its run reports would be the unbacked-execution-claim failure inside the row that files a class about misattributed evidence.",
    "**THIRD SPECIMEN, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT BREAKS THE RUNG: FOUR SILENT GREENS ON THE FLEET, 2026-10-17, runs 39066871919, 39067193189, 39067690307 and 39069382384.** Same split, opposite presentation. WHAT SITS APART HERE: the two 2026-09 specimens are heal-generated-artifacts, the one job that declares a ref differing from the workflow file because it pushes; these four are the MAIN required lane, job v2-bootstrap, on the DEFAULT github.actions/checkout@v4 with NO ref at all -- the instruction and the tree were never declared to be paired, and the pairing never held. MEASURED, PER RUN. Run 39066871919: branch session/sunny-wren-697-roster-import-sweep, head 5003782716, run conclusion success, job v2-bootstrap success, event pull_request. The executed step-37 argv CARRIES the option --reuse-from <workdir>/gunbc-parse-verdict-restore and the step's own header and body print the NEW step name (Walk parse-population reusing the nearest published ancestor's receipts), so the instruction is the NEW one. What ran it is the OLD verb: its stdout is parse-verdict reuse: reused=0 miss_identity_absent=8692 miss_identity_mismatch=0 miss_entry_absent=0 miss_digest_unseen=0 miss_declared_refused=0 -- the six-field shape, and the token ancestor= appears ZERO TIMES in the whole job log. Step conclusion: success. Same on the other three: 9c7c9def31 (session/swift-wren-451, reused=0 miss_identity_absent=8700), 3612d1c513 (session/jolly-cat-787, 8696), 61f2777b72 (session/nimble-koi-194, 8693); 12 identity/receipt restore misses each; ancestor= absent in every log; success every time. THE HEAD/MAIN TEST: all four heads CONTAIN ddbf145d0f (#14291, which added the --reuse-from plumbing) and all four LACK cadb3c63b7 (#14298, which added the ancestor locator and the ancestor= field), while origin/main at the same hour has 2 and 1. So the workflow text came from the merge ref and the verb from the head -- the SAME split as the 2026-09 specimens, on the lane whose checkout ref is github.event.pull_request.head.sha. THE CONTROL, so the shape is not just what this hour prints: run 39074669888, event pull_request, head 0ef028fc9b (which DOES contain cadb3c63b7), prints parse-verdict reuse: ancestor=61271d312574ff0d97bdb65f1fb39c2ce198c35b reused=8664 miss_identity_absent=0 with 0 restore misses. NEW SHAPE, LOTS REUSED, PAIRED. WHY IT IS SILENT AND NOT MERELY UNUSUAL: the walk step's only contract on this line is a shell exit code. Nothing compares the printed field set against the one the instruction specified, so an older verb answering a newer command is indistinguishable from a newer verb with nothing to reuse -- and reused=0 is a LEGITIMATE outcome, which is what makes the specimen so easy to read as fine. It IS fine in the sense section 5 cares about: every module that could not be reused was re-parsed from the head tree, which is the subject this lane judges, so the verdicts are sound. What is lost is the standing the field carries -- ancestor= names which published revision the reuse claim is measured against, and with no ancestor there is no such claim. THE GREEN IS REAL AND THE GREEN IS ALSO UNATTRIBUTED. NOT CLAIMED: that the four heads were intended to lack cadb3c63b7, or that the loud form occurred in these runs. The loud form (unknown option --reuse-from, exit 127 with an empty --compiler, reported by gentle-cat-348 on 2026-10-17 for merry-fox-82 and stern-bat-401) is RECORDED AS ATTESTED AND NOT VERIFIED HERE: those two branches have been deleted and no such run survives to measure. It is the same split one link further along -- an older verb met with the option it does not parse -- and it lands at rung 1, exactly as the two 2026-09 specimens do.",


    "**SCOPE, STATED BECAUSE THE CLASS IS WRITTEN WIDER THAN ITS EVIDENCE.** The general shape is a step whose checkout ref differs from the ref that supplied the workflow file, and it is written at that grain deliberately: the next instance will be whichever step next needs to write back to the branch. But the THREE EXECUTED SPECIMENS ARE NOT ONE JOB: the 2026-09 pair is heal-generated-artifacts, which declares a ref on purpose, and the 2026-10 four are job v2-bootstrap, which declares NO ref and still split. So the population is TWO JOBS AND TWO PRESENTATIONS, and the more common one is the silent one. THE INFERENCE TO REFUSE: that a step whose checkout ref is the triggering sha is paired with the instruction it runs. It is not; that is where the silent greens came from. A reader deciding whether their own red -- or their own GREEN -- is this class should compare the SHAPE a step's output is specified to have against the shape it printed, not assume the instruction and the tree came from the same revision because nothing was said otherwise.",

    "**DISTINCT FROM ITS THREE NEAREST NEIGHBOURS, and the repair law is what separates them.** upstream_carrier_substituted_for_the_consumer_selected_subject is about deriving a CLAIM about a consumer from a carrier the consumer transforms; there the defect is in a measurement and the repair is to measure the composed subject. Here nothing is measured -- the EXECUTOR pairs the two revisions itself, and the repair is in the step. verification_bound_to_the_revision_it_started_on is one tree MOVING across an interval; here the two revisions are fixed for the whole run and differ by construction, so waiting or re-running changes nothing. receipt_names_a_property_not_the_tree_it_holds_of is an expired GREEN that produces no signal at all; this class produces a loud, correctly typed RED whose subject attribution is false, which is a different failure to catch and a different one to trust.",

    "**RUNG: 1 (mitigatable), AND THAT 1 BELONGS TO THE LOUD ARM ONLY.** The 2026-09 pair established 1 correctly -- the line stops, the diagnostic is typed and located, harm bounded to a misattributed red plus the investigation it misdirects -- and the sentence attached to it, that there is no silent wrongness here, was a claim about the CLASS drawn from the loud arm alone. The 2026-10 four falsify THAT SENTENCE, and falsifying it does not raise the rung, it removes the cover the sentence was giving the second path. THE SILENT ARM HAS NO RUNG OF ITS OWN TODAY: nothing stops it, no diagnostic exists on it, and the harm arrives as a green -- so it is not covered by the 1, and claiming 2 for the class before a RED refuses it on the real acceptance path is precisely the rung inflation section 4b(1) calls worse than sitting low. WHAT 2 IS HERE: the reachable CEILING for this arm, stated as reachable because the refusal is expressible in the one place that already holds both halves -- the generated step -- with no model of GitHub's ref supply and no new toolchain. THE ROW MOVES TO 2 WHEN A DISCRIMINATING RED AND AN ACCEPTED POSITIVE CONTROL HAVE EXECUTED, not before. **CEILING: 3 (structurally guaranteed), and not 4, for a stated reason:** which ref supplies a workflow file is GitHub's decision and sits outside the modeled guarantee, so no constructor of ours can make the two-ref pairing unwritable. What IS reachable is that a step cannot emit an unattributed refusal: the invocation is resolved against the tree the step checked out, and an entry that tree does not define refuses by naming BOTH revisions and classifying the cause as an instruction/subject split rather than as a defect in the module it names. **CEILING DROPS TO 2 -- it is a ceiling, NOT the rung, and the row's rung stays where executed evidence puts it.** The old ceiling argument was aimed AT THE LANE and survives untouched: which ref supplies a workflow file is GitHub's decision, outside the modeled guarantee, so no constructor of ours can make the two-ref pairing unwritable. It is still the ceiling FOR THE LANE. But it was the ONLY ceiling this row had ever considered, and the silent arm is a DIFFERENT PATH WITH ITS OWN, REACHABLE CEILING: no property of GitHub is needed to make a generated step refuse an output shape its own instruction did not specify. So the class has two paths at two ceilings -- the lane's pairing at 3, unreachable, and the step's output contract at 2, reachable -- and the row's climbable headroom is the reachable one. THE SECOND CEILING BEING REACHABLE IS WHY THE RUNG MOVED AND THE LANE ARGUMENT DID NOT. **NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability and not an artifact: a step whose entry point is resolved from the revision it checked out, with the refusal carrying both revision identities -- AND, on the silent arm, a generated step that cannot pass a subject that answers it in a shape its instruction never specified.** What does NOT satisfy it: making this one job check out the merge ref, which it cannot do and still push; renaming or restoring the function; or adding a note telling readers to merge main, which is guidance rather than a mechanism. NOR DOES IT: a check on the OUTPUT ALONE -- refuse if the counts line lacks ancestor= -- which is rung 2 and a valid containment but is a PROXY, one per symptom, that passes any other old-vs-new shape mismatch and hard-codes one field name; the step's own generated instruction, which is where both halves of the fact already meet. **RECOGNITION RULE, BOTH ARMS. LOUD: when a job refuses naming an entry point, function or path that is absent from the branch, ask which revision supplied the COMMAND and which supplied the TREE, and demand both as immutable shas.** **SILENT: when a step passes but prints a field set the instruction does not specify -- a counts line missing a field the new verb is known to print, a header naming a step the checked-out tree never generated -- the same two questions apply, and the tell is that the printed shape is the OLD one while the step's own header is the NEW one in the same log.** The tell is that the refusal's own subject has no history on the branch at all -- git log -S on the branch is empty -- while git show <default-branch>:<workflow file> contains the invocation. Two refs, one of them never named in the error.",
    "**RUNG: THE SILENT ARM NOW CARRIES 2, AND THE TRIGGER THAT MOVED IT IS THE ONE THIS ROW WROTE FOR ITSELF.** The row said THE ROW MOVES TO 2 WHEN A DISCRIMINATING RED AND AN ACCEPTED POSITIVE CONTROL HAVE EXECUTED, not before; §4b(1) makes claiming it earlier worse than sitting low, and it was duly held at 1 across two commits while the mechanism was still a design. WHAT EXECUTED, and where a reader can audit it: the guard script was taken out of the GENERATED .github/workflows/witnesses.yml (not written by hand into a test), and run under bash -e -o pipefail against each of the four measured heads' OWN committed workflow file. All four exit 1 naming StampAbsent -- the silent greens of runs 39066871919, 39067193189, 39067690307 and 39069382384 are now refusals on those exact bytes. The controls executed too: a head carrying the current stamp proceeds; a hand-written workflow with no workflow-level stamp refuses StampUnreadable naming the file and the remedy; a tree carrying a DIFFERENT stamp refuses StampMismatch naming BOTH values; and the DISCRIMINATING pair -- a PR changing the contract itself, head and merge ref both carrying the new stamp, proceeds, which is what separates a guard from a wall. WHAT THE 2 IS AND IS NOT: rung 2 is the step-output-contract path, whose ceiling this row already recorded as REACHABLE, and it is now occupied by a mechanism that is tested by running it. It is NOT the lane's pairing at 3, which stays unreachable for the stated reason -- which ref supplies a workflow file is GitHub's decision, outside the modeled guarantee. It is also NOT a claim that the fleet is now refusing: the four greens were observed on main's own lane, and the first real refusal on a real acceptance path lands when this reaches main and a PR run stops. Until then the row is 2 with that plainly said, and the loud arm keeps the 1 the 2026-09 pair established for it.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
